{:edb/db-before-t 1283 :edb/db-after-t 1284 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534596 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:58:11.591Z" 13194139534596 true] [17592186082957 1100 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u^2 +u}$." 13194139534596 false] [17592186082957 1100 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u^2 +u}.$" 13194139534596 true] [17592186082957 1145 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u}  =\\dfrac{5}{u(u+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big( 3u, \\:u+1, \\: u(u+1) \\big) = 3u(u+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $u=-1$ and $u=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{u+1}-\\frac{1}{3u} & =\\frac{5}{u(u+1)} \\\\ 3u(u+1)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{u+1}-\\frac{1}{3u}) & =3u(u+1)\\cdot \\frac{5}{u(u+1)} \\\\ \\frac{9u(u+1)}{u+1}-\\frac{3u(u+1)}{3u} & =\\frac{15u(u+1)}{u(u+1)} \\\\ \\frac{9u\\cdot \\cancel{(u+1)}}{\\cancel{u+1}}-\\frac{\\cancel{3u}\\cdot (u+1)}{\\cancel{3u}} & =\\frac{15\\cdot \\cancel{u(u+1)}}{\\cancel{u(u+1)}} \\\\ 9u-(u+1) & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $u,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9u-(u+1) & =15 \\\\ 9u-u-1 & =15 \\\\ 8u & =16 \\\\ u & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\neq -1$ and $2\\neq 0,$ we conclude that $u=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $u=2.$" 13194139534596 true] [17592186082957 1145 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u(u+1)}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(3u, \\:u+1, \\: u(u+1) \\big) = 3u(u+1)$.\n- The values $u=-1$ and $u=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{u + 1} - \\frac{1}{3u} &= (5)/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n3u(u + 1) \\cdot (\\frac{3}{u + 1} - \\frac{1}{3u}) &= 3u(u + 1) \\cdot (5)/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n(9u(u + 1))/(u + 1) - (3u(u + 1))/(3u) &= (15u(u + 1))/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n(9u \\cdot (u + 1))/(u + 1) - (3u \\cdot (u + 1))/(3u) &= (15 \\cdot u(u + 1))/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n9u - (u + 1) &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $u$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9u - (u + 1) &= 15 \\\\\n9u - u - 1 &= 15 \\\\\n8u &= 16 \\\\\nu &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n2\\neq -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $u=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $u=2$." 13194139534596 false] [17592186082958 1100 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2}  = \\dfrac{2}{k^2-5k+6}.$" 13194139534596 true] [17592186082958 1100 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2} = \\dfrac{2}{k^2-5k+6}$." 13194139534596 false] [17592186082958 1145 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2}  = \\dfrac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big( k-2, k-3, (k-2)(k-3) \\big) = (k-2)(k-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $k=2$ and $k=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal to zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{k-3}-\\frac{2}{k-2} & =\\frac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ (k-2)(k-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{k-3}-\\frac{2}{k-2}) & =(k-2)(k-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(k-2)(k-3)}{k-3}-\\frac{2(k-2)(k-3)}{k-2} & =\\frac{2(k-2)(k-3)}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(k-2)\\cdot \\cancel{(k-3)}}{\\cancel{k-3}}-\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(k-2)}\\cdot (k-3)}{\\cancel{k-2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(k-2)(k-3)}}{\\cancel{(k-2)(k-3)}} \\\\ 3(k-2)-2(k-3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(k-2)-2(k-3) & =2 \\\\ 3k-6-2k+6 & =2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $k=2$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139534596 true] [17592186082958 1145 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2} = \\dfrac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(k-2, k-3, (k-2)(k-3) \\big) = (k-2)(k-3)$.\n- The values $k=2$ and $k=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal to zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{k - 3} - \\frac{2}{k - 2} &= (2)/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(k - 2)(k - 3) \\cdot (\\frac{3}{k - 3} - \\frac{2}{k - 2}) &= (k - 2)(k - 3) \\cdot (2)/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(3(k - 2)(k - 3))/(k - 3) - (2(k - 2)(k - 3))/(k - 2) &= (2(k - 2)(k - 3))/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(3(k - 2) \\cdot (k - 3))/(k - 3) - (2 \\cdot (k - 2) \\cdot (k - 3))/(k - 2) &= (2 \\cdot (k - 2)(k - 3))/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n3(k - 2) - 2(k - 3) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(k - 2) - 2(k - 3) &= 2 \\\\\n3k - 6 - 2k + 6 &= 2 \\\\\nk &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $k=2$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139534596 false] [17592186083049 1100 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{4} {x - 2} - \\dfrac{8} {x(x - 2)} = \\dfrac {3} {x}$." 13194139534596 false] [17592186083049 1100 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{4} {x - 2} - \\dfrac{8} {x(x - 2)} = \\dfrac {3} {x}.$" 13194139534596 true] [17592186083049 1145 "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big(x-2,\\: x(x-2),\\: x \\big) = x(x-2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x = 0$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{8}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ x(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{8}{x(x-2)}) & =x(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x(x-2)}{x-2}-\\frac{8x(x-2)}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3x(x-2)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x-2}}-\\frac{8\\cancel{x(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x(x-2)}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{x}(x-2)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 4x-8 & =3(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-8 & =3(x-2) \\\\ 4x-8 & =3x-6 \\\\ 4x-3x & =-6+8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=2$ is not a valid solution, we conclude that our equation has no solutions." 13194139534596 true] [17592186083049 1145 "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(x-2,\\: x(x-2),\\: x \\big) = x(x-2)$.\n- The values $x = 0$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{x - 2} - (8)/(x(x - 2)) &= \\frac{3}{x} \\\\\nx(x - 2) \\cdot (\\frac{4}{x - 2} - (8)/(x(x - 2))) &= x(x - 2) \\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\\n(4x(x - 2))/(x - 2) - (8x(x - 2))/(x(x - 2)) &= (3x(x - 2))/(x) \\\\\n(4x(x - 2))/(x - 2) - (8x(x - 2))/(x(x - 2)) &= (3x(x - 2))/(x) \\\\\n4x - 8 &= 3(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 8 &= 3(x - 2) \\\\\n4x - 8 &= 3x - 6 \\\\\n4x - 3x &= -6 + 8 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=2$ is not a valid solution, we conclude that our equation has no solutions." 13194139534596 false] [17592186083156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297837 13194139534596 true] [17592186083156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297844 13194139534596 true] [17592186083156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297858 13194139534596 true] [17592186083157 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297830 13194139534596 true] [17592186083157 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297851 13194139534596 true] [17592186083157 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297871 13194139534596 true] [17592186083157 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297875 13194139534596 true] [17592186083245 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297861 13194139534596 true] [17592186083245 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297865 13194139534596 true] [17592186083245 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297868 13194139534596 true] [17592186083407 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534596 true] [17592186083407 1100 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{y + 5} + \\frac{1}{2y} = \\frac{5}{2y^{2} + 10y}$." 13194139534596 false] [17592186083407 1100 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}=\\frac{5}{2{y}^{2}+10y}.$" 13194139534596 true] [17592186083407 1145 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}=\\frac{5}{2y(y+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(y+5,\\,2y,\\,2y(y+5))=2y(y+5).\n$$\n\n- The values $y=0$ and $y=-5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y} & =\\frac{5}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ 2y(y+5)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}) & =2y(y+5)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{6y(y+5)}{y+5}+\\frac{2y(y+5)}{2y} & =\\frac{5\\cdot 2y(y+5)}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{6y\\cancel{(y+5)}}{\\cancel{y+5}}+\\frac{\\cancel{2y}(y+5)}{\\cancel{2y}} & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2y(y+5)}}{\\cancel{2y(y+5)}} \\\\ 6y+(y+5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+(y+5) & =5 \\\\ 7y+5 & =5 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $y=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139534596 true] [17592186083572 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534596 true] [17592186083572 1100 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{z - 1} - (3)/(z(z - 1)) = \\frac{2}{z}$." 13194139534596 false] [17592186083572 1100 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{z-1}-\\frac{3}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{2}{z}.$" 13194139534596 true] [17592186083572 1145 "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(z,\\,z-1,\\,z(z-1))=z(z-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $z=0$ and $z=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{z-1}-\\frac{3}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2}{z} \\\\ z(z-1)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{z-1}-\\frac{3}{z(z-1)}) & =z(z-1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{z} \\\\ \\frac{3z(z-1)}{z-1}-\\frac{3z(z-1)}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2z(z-1)}{z} \\\\ \\frac{3z\\cdot \\cancel{(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z-1}}-\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{z(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z(z-1)}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{z}\\cdot (z-1)}{\\cancel{z}} \\\\ 3z-3 & =2z-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $z,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-3 & =2z-2 \\\\ z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $z=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139534596 true] [17592186297830 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297831 13194139534596 true] [17592186297830 1089 "q-117687" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297830 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534596 true] [17592186297830 1100 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+m-12}.$" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297830 1113 #uuid "aa8f9db1-a194-5257-abcf-d7a9a1fa6588" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297830 1145 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m+4,\\,m-3,\\,(m+4)(m-3))=(m+4)(m-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $m=-4$ and $m=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3} & =\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ (m+4)(m-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}) & =(m+4)(m-3)\\cdot \\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{m+4}+\\frac{(m+4)(m-3)}{m-3} & =\\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)}(m-3)}{\\cancel{m+4}}+\\frac{(m+4)\\cancel{(m-3)}}{\\cancel{m-3}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}}{\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}} \\\\ 3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3 \\\\ 4m-5 & =3 \\\\ 4m & =8 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\ne -4$ and $2\\ne 3,$ we conclude that $m=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $m=2.$" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297832 13194139534596 true] [17592186297831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297833 13194139534596 true] [17592186297831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297834 13194139534596 true] [17592186297831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297835 13194139534596 true] [17592186297831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297836 13194139534596 true] [17592186297831 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534596 true] [17592186297831 1041 "selection" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297831 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297832 13194139534596 true] [17592186297831 1110 #uuid "1223711f-0647-501c-a736-a04981473aeb" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297832 1034 "m=2" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534596 true] [17592186297832 1111 #uuid "1994042f-fdaa-5194-9330-5f50cb8e61a7" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297833 1034 "m=\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297833 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534596 true] [17592186297833 1111 #uuid "14a3b11a-5be3-57d9-bb5b-fda637e3aa03" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297834 1034 "m=1" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534596 true] [17592186297834 1111 #uuid "62473451-942f-5098-b544-fc42e2e35dbb" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297835 1034 "m=-6" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297835 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534596 true] [17592186297835 1111 #uuid "58fb4430-a757-5f9b-8511-4076eceea268" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297836 1034 "No solutions" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297836 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534596 true] [17592186297836 1111 #uuid "60b2c9d7-fbd8-500c-beb5-d3f551d495bc" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297837 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297838 13194139534596 true] [17592186297837 1089 "q-118123" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297837 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534596 true] [17592186297837 1100 "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{{z}^{2}-z}=\\frac{1}{2z}.$" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297837 1113 #uuid "4a5ccbb7-a0cc-5e63-9faa-a0929882fb83" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297837 1145 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{1}{2z}.\n$$\n\nWe make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(z-1,\\,2z,\\,z(z-1))=2z(z-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $z=0$ and $z=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ 2z(z-1)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}) & =2z(z-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z(z-1)}{z-1}-\\frac{10z(z-1)}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2z(z-1)}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z\\cdot \\cancel{(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z-1}}-\\frac{10\\cdot \\cancel{z(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z(z-1)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2z}\\cdot (z-1)}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ 10z-10 & =z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $z,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10z-10 & =z-1 \\\\ 9z & =9 \\\\ z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $z=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139534596 true] [17592186297838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297839 13194139534596 true] [17592186297838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297840 13194139534596 true] [17592186297838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297841 13194139534596 true] [17592186297838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297842 13194139534596 true] [17592186297838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297843 13194139534596 true] [17592186297838 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534596 true] [17592186297838 1041 "selection" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297838 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297842 13194139534596 true] [17592186297838 1110 #uuid "1ba1fedc-1332-5481-9e0a-b8e832baed58" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297839 1034 "z=3" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297839 110