d-b2b3-405b7246e196" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297861 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297862 13194139534596 true] [17592186297861 1089 "q-276457" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297861 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534596 true] [17592186297861 1100 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h}=\\frac{1}{h(3h+2)}.$\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297861 1113 #uuid "724ac140-4e92-5794-904f-c50fc9c01137" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297861 1145 "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3h+2,\\,5h,\\,h(3h+2))=5h(3h+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $h=0$ and $h=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h} & =\\frac{1}{h(3h+2)} \\\\ 5h(3h+2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h}) & =5h(3h+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{h(3h+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5h(3h+2)}{3h+2}+\\frac{5h(3h+2)}{5h} & =\\frac{5h(3h+2)}{h(3h+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5h\\cancel{(3h+2)}}{\\cancel{3h+2}}+\\frac{\\cancel{5h}(3h+2)}{\\cancel{5h}} & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{h(3h+2)}}{\\cancel{h(3h+2)}} \\\\ 5h+(3h+2) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $h,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5h+(3h+2) & =5 \\\\ 5h+3h+2 & =5 \\\\ 8h & =3 \\\\ h & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{8}\\ne 0$ and $\\frac{3}{8}\\ne -\\frac{2}{3},$ we conclude that $h=\\frac{3}{8}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $h=\\frac{3}{8}.$" 13194139534596 true] [17592186297862 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297863 13194139534596 true] [17592186297862 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297864 13194139534596 true] [17592186297862 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534596 true] [17592186297862 1041 "field-1" 13194139534596 true] [1759218